To find the temperature \( T \) at which the number of atoms in the first excited state is a given percentage of the number of atoms in the ground state, we use the Boltzmann equation:

\$\$\nT = \frac{E_2 - E_1}{k \ln \left[ \left(\frac{g_2}{g_1}\right) / \left(\frac{N_2}{N_1}\right) \right]}
\$\$
\nwhere \( E_1 \) and \( E_2 \) are the energies of the ground state and the first excited state, respectively, \( g_1 \) and \( g_2 \) are the degeneracies of the ground state and the first excited state, and \( N_1 \) and \( N_2 \) are the number of atoms in the ground state and the first excited state, respectively.
\nFor the first excited state (\( n = 2 \)) and the ground state (\( n = 1 \)), we have:

\$\$\ng_1 = 2 (1)^2 = 2, \quad g_2 = 2 (2)^2 = 8, \quad E_1 = -13.6 \text{ eV}, \quad E_2 = -3.40 \text{ eV}
\$\$

### Case 1: \( N_2 / N_1 = 0.01 \)
\nSubstituting the values into the Boltzmann equation:

\$\$\nT = \frac{-3.40 \text{ eV} - (-13.6 \text{ eV})}{k \ln \left[ \left(\frac{8}{2}\right) / 0.01 \right]}
\$\$

\$\$\nT = \frac{10.2 \text{ eV}}{k \ln \left[ 4 / 0.01 \right]}
\$\$

\$\$\nT = \frac{10.2 \text{ eV}}{k \ln 400}
\$\$
\nUsing \( k = 8.617 \times 10^{-5} \text{ eV/K} \):

\$\$\nT = \frac{10.2 \text{ eV}}{8.617 \times 10^{-5} \text{ eV/K} \cdot 6.00}
\$\$

\$\$\nT = \frac{10.2 \text{ eV}}{0.000517 \text{ eV/K}}
\$\$

\$\$\nT = 1.97 \times 10^4 \text{ K}
\$\$

### Case 2: \( N_2 / N_1 = 0.10 \)
\nSubstituting the values into the Boltzmann equation:

\$\$\nT = \frac{-3.40 \text{ eV} - (-13.6 \text{ eV})}{k \ln \left[ \left(\frac{8}{2}\right) / 0.10 \right]}
\$\$

\$\$\nT = \frac{10.2 \text{ eV}}{k \ln \left[ 4 / 0.10 \right]}
\$\$

\$\$\nT = \frac{10.2 \text{ eV}}{k \ln 40}
\$\$
\nUsing \( k = 8.617 \times 10^{-5} \text{ eV/K} \):

\$\$\nT = \frac{10.2 \text{ eV}}{8.617 \times 10^{-5} \text{ eV/K} \cdot 3.69}
\$\$

\$\$\nT = \frac{10.2 \text{ eV}}{0.000319 \text{ eV/K}}
\$\$

\$\$\nT = 3.21 \times 10^4 \text{ K}
\$\$
\nThe temperatures are \(\boxed{1.97 \times 10^4 \text{ K}}\) and \(\boxed{3.21 \times 10^4 \text{ K}}\).

